A functionalized metal-organic framework decorated with $\mathbf{O}^{-}$ groups showing excellent performance for lead(II) removal from aqueous solution†
5 Received (in XXX, XXX) xxxxx  2017, Accepted xxxxx  2017 First published on the web xxxxx  2017 DOI: 10.1039/c000000x
Heavy metal ions are highly toxic and widely spread as environmental pollutants. New strategies are being developed to efficiently remove these toxic ions. Herein, we take the intrinsic advantages 10 of metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) and develop a porous $Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{II})$ -based MOF decorated with $0^{-}$ groups for the removal of $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ . Benefiting from its multiple porosity, sufficient adsorption sites and strong affinity, the activated MOF material exhibits ultrahigh $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ uptake capacity $(616.64~\mathrm{mg}$ $\mathbf{g}^{-1})$ ), surpassing all those of reported MOF adsorbents. Moreover, it can selectively capture $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ with high efficiency $(>99.27\%)$ against background ions. Even in the presence of high 15 concentration of competitive ions, such as ${\mathrm{Ca}}^{2+}$ or $\ensuremath{\mathbf{M}}\ensuremath{\mathbf{g}}^{2+}$ , effective removal $(>99.21\%)$ can also be achieved in a short time. The excellent removal performance demonstrates the strong electrostatic attraction and coordination interaction between the highly accessible $0^{-}$ groups and $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ . The possible adsorption mechanism was systematically verified by zeta potential, FT-IR and XPS studies. Our work reveals the enormous potential of functionalized MOFs as an appealing platform 20 to construct sorbent materials.
## Introduction
Lead $(\mathrm{Pb}^{2+})$ , a prevalent and bio-accumulative heavy metal, has been recognized as one of the most toxic metals.1 With the rapid growth of industrialization and urbanization, $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ discharge has 25 become a serious environmental concern.2 At present, available clean water is limited and $90\%$ of the available fresh water will be consumed by 2025.3 Almost all countries strive to remove $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ from potable water in order to reduce levels of elevated blood $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ in children.4 Owing to the fact, the removal of $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ 30 from water is critical in terms of protection of public health and environment. Many efforts have been dedicated to purify wastewater to get more available clean water. However, an efficient, cost-effective, robust and handy technology for the decontamination of water is urgently needed. The adsorption 35 technique for removing heavy metal ions has gained extensive attention considering easy operation, eco-friendliness and costeffectiveness.1,5 Some effective adsorbents have been continuously developed and improved, including zeolites,6 carbon materials,7 clay minerals,8 nanomaterials9,10 and chelating 40 polymers.11 Meanwhile, those sorbents face some challenges, such as low capacity, moderate affinity/selectivity, difficulties in separation, lack of structural and functional tunability,12 which have largely limited the effectiveness for $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ removal.
Therefore, there is an increasing interest in developing more 45 efficient absorbents for removal of $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ from aqueous solution.
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), constructed by metal ions or metal clusters and organic ligands through coordination bonds, are considered as the favorable platform for the adsorption applications,13-15 because of their high surface area, tunable 50 chemical composition, variable pore size distribution and exposed active sites.16-18 By ligand modifications or MOF postfunctionalization, various functional groups can be purposefully incorporated into the pores of MOFs, giving rise to more active sites for facile adsorption. The functionalized MOFs decorated 55 with neutral groups, such as thiol/thioether/hydroxyl/azine/sulphur-functional groups, were exploited for the removal of $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ , ${\mathrm{UO}_{2}}^{+}$ , $\mathrm{Cd}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}$ , et.al from aqueous solution.5,19-29 Two elaborately constructed sulphurfunctionalized MOFs, FJI-H9 and FJI-H12, could selectively 60 remove $\mathrm{Cd}^{2+}$ and $\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}$ from water with high uptake capacities 1 $286~\mathrm{mg~g^{-1}}$ for $\mathrm{Cd}^{2+}$ , $439.8~\mathrm{mg~g^{-1}}$ for $\mathrm{Hg}^{2+},$ ).28,29 Nonetheless, the introduction of negatively charged groups into MOFs for highly efficient removal of heavy metal ions has been rarely reported.
65 Herein, a functionalized MOF decorated with negatively charged $\mathrm{o^{-}}$ groups was designed and applied for the removal of $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ from aqueous solution (Scheme 1). Based on the design, the negative charges of the $\mathrm{~o^{-}~}$ groups generate electrostatic interaction with $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ , which acts as a driving force in the 70 adsorption process. Secondly, the $\mathrm{o^{-}}$ groups also worked as active sites to form coordination bonds with $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ . Thirdly, as a Lewis acid, $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ has lower hydration energy and larger ionic radius, which could readily accept electrons from the Lewis base of $0^{-}$ groups. As a result, the $0^{-}$ groups show significant affinity
and high selectivity for $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ . Fourthly, the multiple porosity densely populated with $\mathrm{o^{-}}$ groups would endow $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ with fast sorption and high removal efficiency. In the study, we synthesized a three-dimensional (3D) porous framework 5 $\{{[\mathrm{{Zn}_{3}L_{3}(B P E)_{1.5}]\cdot4.5{\mathrm{DMF}}}}\}_{n}$ (1, $\mathrm{H}_{2}L=4{,}4^{\prime}$ -azoxydibenzoic acid, $\mathrm{BPE}=\mathrm{bis}(4$ -pyridyl)ethylene, ${\bf D M F}=N,N.$ -dimethylformamide) functionalized with $0^{-}$ groups for the removal of $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ . The activated MOF material $[\mathrm{Zn}_{3}\mathrm{L}_{3}(\mathrm{BPE})_{1.5}]_{n}$ (1a) exhibited an ultrahigh uptake capacity $616.64~\mathrm{mg~g^{-1})}$ ), enormous affinity $(K_{d}$ 10 $10^{6}\mathrm{mLg^{-1})}$ and high removal efficiency for $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ . The adsorption mechanism was revealed by zeta potential, FT-IR and XPS studies. To the best of our knowledge, we first introduce the functional groups with negative charges into the pores of MOFs for removal of heavy metal ions with excellent performance.
## Experimental
## Materials and apparatus
The ligand $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{L}$ was synthesized as literature method.30 All other 20 reagents and chemicals were of analytical grade and obtained from commercial sources. Powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) data were collected on PANalytical X’Pert PRO MPD system (PW3040/60). Fourier transform infrared (FT-IR) spectrums were conducted on a Thermo Nicolet iS50 spectrometer. Scanning 25 electron microscopy (SEM) images were taken on a Hitachi SU8010 instrument. X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) were obtained with a Thermo Escalab 250 spectrometer with monochromated $\mathrm{Al}_{K a}$ excitation. The zeta potential were determined using dynamic light scattering (DLS) on Malvern 30 Instruments Nanosizer-ZS. Thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) were carried out on a Netzsch STA-449F3 thermogravimetric analyzer in the nitrogen atmosphere at a heating rate of $10~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ $\operatorname*{min}^{-1}$ . Simultaneous inductively coupled plasma optical emission spectrometry (ICP-OES) on a PerkinElmer Optima 8000 35 instrument was used to determine the concentration of metal ion in aqueous solution.
## Preparation of $\{{\bf[Z n_{3}L_{3}(B P E)_{1.5}]^{.}}4.5{\bf D}{\bf M}{\bf F}\}_{n}$ (1)
$\mathrm{Zn(NO_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6H_{2}O}$ ( $14.9~\mathrm{mg}$ , $0.05\mathrm{\mmol};$ , $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{L}$ $\mathrm{14.3~mg}$ , 0.05 mmol), BPE $4.6~\mathrm{\mg}$ , 0.025 mmol) and $6~\mathrm{mL}$ DMF were 40 placed in $10~\mathrm{mL}$ vial. The mixture was stirred until complete dissolution, and then it was kept in an oven with the temperature of $100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . Orange block crystals of 1 were obtained, washed with DMF, and dried at room
temperature. Yield: $8.8~\mathrm{mg}$ ( $40\%$ , based on $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{L}$ ). Anal. Calcd. 45 for $\mathrm{C_{73.5}H_{70.5}N_{13.5}Z n_{3}O_{19.5}}$ : C, 53.46; H, 4.30; N, 11.45. Found: C, 53.38; H, 4.07; N, 11.07.
## Preparation of $[\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n}_{3}\mathbf{L}_{3}(\mathbf{BPE})_{1.5}]_{n}$ (1a, the activated 1)
MOF 1a was prepared by heating MOF 1 at $140~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under vacuum $(24\mathrm{h})$ to remove the encapsulated DMF guests before 50 adsorption studies. As confirmed by PXRD, TG and elemental analysis, 1a retained the same framework of 1, but without DMF solvent molecule in the cavity. Anal. Calcd. for $\mathrm{C_{60}H_{39}N_{9}Z n_{3}O_{15}}$ : C, 54.50; H, 2.97; N, 9.53. Found: C, 54.21; H, 3.22; N, 9.15.
## 55 X-Ray data collection and structure determination
Single $\mathrm{\Delta}\mathrm{X}$ -ray diffraction intensities of crystals were collected on a CCD diffractometer at $153\mathrm{~K~}$ . All diffractometers were equipped with a graphite monochromated Mo-Kα radiation $\gimel$ $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}0.71073\$ ). The structure was solved by direct method and 60 expanded with Fourier technique. All calculations were performed with SHELXL-97 package.31 In 1, two azoxy groups of $\mathrm{L}^{2-}$ ligand were found to be disordered over two positions. All $\mathrm{~H~}$ atoms in 1 were placed in geometrically idealized positions and constrained to ride on their parent 65 atoms. Moreover, the diffused electron densities resulting from these residual solvent molecules were removed from the data set using the SQUEEZE routine of PLATON and refined further using the data generated.32 The formula of $\{{[\mathrm{{Zn}_{3}L_{3}(B P E)_{1.5}]\cdot4.5{\mathrm{DMF}}}}\}_{n}$ was derived from 70 thermogravimetric characterization. The crystal data for 1 was summarized as follows: $\mathrm{C}_{60}\mathrm{H}_{39}\mathrm{N}_{9}\mathrm{O}_{15}\mathrm{Zn}_{3}$ , $\begin{array}{r l r}{\mathbf{M}\mathbf{r}}&{{}=}&{1322.17.}\end{array}$ , monoclinic, space group $C2/c$ , $a=19.604$ (4) Å, $b=28.079$ (6) $\mathring\mathrm{A}$ , $c=32.670(7)\mathrm{~\AA~}$ , $\alpha=90^{\circ}$ , $\beta=94.47(3)^{\circ}$ , $\gamma=90^{\circ}$ , $V=$ 17929(7) $\mathring{\mathbf{A}}^{3}$ , $Z=8$ , $D_{\mathrm{c}}=0.980\ \mathrm{g\cm}^{-3}$ , $F(000)=5376$ and $\mu=$ 75 $0.846~\mathrm{mm^{-1}}$ , 84732 reflections collected, 15810 unique $\langle R_{\mathrm{int}}=$ 0.0546). $R_{1}~=~0.0623$ , $w R_{2}~=~0.1871$ and $\mathrm{~{~\bf~S~}~}=\mathrm{~\bf~1.093~}$ . Crystallographic data have been submitted to the Cambridge Structural Database with deposition number CCDC 1536031.
## Adsorption studies
80 All the adsorption experiments were carried out at $25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ using $10~\mathrm{mg}$ of MOF 1a and $80~\mathrm{mL}$ of $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ standard solution. The $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ solution was prepared by dissolving $\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}$ in deionized water and diluted to desired concentration. To study the adsorption kinetics, adsorption experiments were 85 performed at $\mathrm{pH}6.0$ ( $10\ \mathrm{ppm}\ \mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ solution) under continuous stirring. The $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ concentrations of samples were measured at given time intervals by ICP-OES spectrometer. The adsorption isotherm experiments were investigated by adding $10~\mathrm{\mg}$ MOF 1a into $80\mathrm{~mL~Pb}^{2+}$ solutions with different 90 concentrations to reach adsorption equilibrium within 180 min. The adsorption capacity $q_{t}$ $l_{t}~(\mathrm{mg}~\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ and the removal efficiency were obtained from the following equation:
$$
q_{\mathrm{t}}={\frac{\left(C_{0}-C_{t}\right)V}{m}}
$$
where $C_{0}$ $(\mathrm{mg~L}^{-1})$ ) and $C_{e}$ $\mathrm{{(mg~L^{-1}}},$ ) are the initial and equilibrium $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ concentrations, respectively; $C_{t}$ $(\mathrm{mg~L^{-1}};$ is ${\mathrm{Pb}}^{2+}$ concentration at time t (min); $V$ (L) is the volume of the solution, and $m$ (g) is the dry weight of adsorbent.
Furthermore, the desorption test was carried out by 5 immersing the ${\mathrm{Pb}}^{2+}$ loaded MOF in $80~\mathrm{mL}$ of ${\mathrm{HNO}}_{3}$ solution $(0.1\mathrm{\mmol\L^{-1}}.$ ) at $25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . Then it was washed three times with water and dried by vacuum at $140^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . The desorbed $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ was evaluated by the same method used in the adsorption studies.
## 10 Results and discussion
## Characterization of 1 and 1a
Single-crystal $\mathrm{\Delta}\mathrm{X}$ -ray diffraction study indicates that MOF 1 crystallizes in the monoclinic space group $C2/c$ . The framework is composed of paddlewheel dinuclear $Z\mathrm{n}_{2}\mathrm{(COO)}_{4}$ secondary 15 building units that are bridged by $\mathrm{L}^{2-}$ ligands and further pillared by BPE to construct an interpenetrating 3D framework (Fig. 1). Although the interpenetration occurs, two kinds of pores are still present in MOF 1 (Figs. 1c-d), which are calculated by the Platon program31 to have an effective solvent accessible volume of 20 8462.8 $\mathring{\mathbf{A}}^{3}$ per unit cell $(47.2\%$ of the total cell volume). As expected, the pores in MOF 1 are decorated by a large number of $\mathrm{o^{-}}$ sites, resulting in the functionalized pores. These functionalized pores will certainly be favorable for capture heavy metal ions.
25 Thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) of 1 displays major weight loss $(20.2\%)$ of DMF guest molecules at $40{-}222^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (Fig. S1). So, the as-synthesized 1 was heated at $140^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under vacuum for $24\mathrm{h}$ to generate the activated sample 1a. The TGA of 1a showed that the DMF molecules were completely removed (Fig. S1), which 30 suggested it would be potential sorbent. The TGA together with the elemental analysis confirmed that the chemical formula of 1a was $[Z{\bf n}_{3}{\bf L}_{3}({\bf B P E})_{1.5}]_{n}$ .
To examine the permanent porosity of 1a, gas sorption isotherms were investigated (Fig. S2). Unexpectedly, almost no 35 ${\bf N}_{2}$ adsorption at $77~\mathrm{{K}}$ compared to $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption at $195\mathrm{~K~}$ which can be possibly attributed to the different kinetic diameters of $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ $(3.64\mathrm{~\AA~})$ and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ $(3.30\mathrm{~\AA~}$ , easier diffusion into the micropores). Similar ${\bf N}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sorption behaviors have been observed in many other flexible MOF materials.33-38 The 40 Brunauer-Emmett-Teller surface area of 1a is calculated to be $82.5~\mathrm{~m}^{2}~\mathrm{~g}^{-1}$ based on the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption isotherm. This is probably due to a higher diffusion barrier imposed on the 1D pore for the flexible MOF after desolvation and the corresponding structural contraction.33,34
45 As illustrated in Fig. S3, the X-ray diffraction peaks obtained from 1a were sharp, indicating the crystalline nature of the desolvated phase. The low-angle Bragg’s reflection in 1 vanished in 1a. According to the literature, there is a slight shrinkage of the framework 1a after guest molecules removal from 1.38-41 Soaking 50 1a in DMF for $24\ensuremath{\mathrm{~h~}}$ generated 1', the low-angle Bragg’s reflection reappeared and the PXRD was almost the same with that of 1 (Fig. S3), which indicates that 1a is a flexible MOF and the framework did not collapse during this process. These results can be explained by the breathing behavior which has been 55 extensively studied over the past decades (Scheme S1).34,35,41,42
For the flexible interpenetrated frameworks, subtle differences of guest content and composition will lead to different structures, and this transformation is reversible.33,36,39,43,44 Interestingly, a slight difference was observed in the PXRD data simulated from 60 single crystal data 1 at $153\mathrm{~K~}$ and collected at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ (Fig. S3). It can be attributed that the temperature induced structural changes for the flexible interpenetrated frameworks34,39 and also resulted in different unit cell parameters ( $153\mathrm{~K~}$ , $a=19.604\mathrm{~\AA~}$ , $b=28.079$ $\mathring\mathrm{A}$ , $c=32.670\mathrm{~\AA~}$ , $\alpha=90^{\circ}$ , $\beta=94.47^{\circ}$ , $\gamma=90^{\circ}$ , $V=17929\mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{3}$ ; 298 65 K, $a=21.79\textrm{\AA}$ , $b=26.94\mathrm{~\AA~}$ , $c=33.00\mathrm{~\AA~}$ , $\ a=90^{\circ}$ , $\beta=92.24^{\circ}$ , γ $=90^{\circ}$ , $V=19358\mathring{\mathbf{A}}^{3}$ ).
To elucidate solvent induced breathing behavior,33 1 was immersed in water $(24\mathrm{~h})$ , and the single crystallinity of the resulted $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}.$ -exchanged 1 $\left(\mathbf{1}{\cdot}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\right)$ was not good. However, 70 immersing $\mathbf{1}{\cdot}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ in DMF for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ , $\mathbf{1-H_{2}O-D M F}$ with better crystal quality was obtained and the unit cell parameters were very similar to that of 1 $298\mathrm{~K~}$ , Table S1). So the structural transformation is reversible, and it is directly evidenced by the single crystal images (Fig. S4). To reveal the structural change in 75 the process of solvent-exchange, we tried to determine the crystal structures of various solvent-exchanged MOFs. Fortunately, we got the single-crystal structure of $\mathrm{CHCl}_{3}$ -exchanged 1 $\begin{array}{r}{(\mathbf{1-CHCl}_{3}),}\end{array}$ at $153\mathrm{~K~}$ (Table S2), which retained the original metal-ligand connectivity. While the total cell volume decreased from 17929 80 $\mathring{\mathbf{A}}^{3}$ for 1 $(153~\mathrm{K})$ to $17753\mathrm{~\AA~}^{3}$ for $\mathbf{1-CHCl}_{3}$ (Table S2), indicating a slight contraction of the square grid (Fig. S5). After soaking 1- $\mathrm{CHCl}_{3}$ in DMF, the unit cell parameters of the generated 1- $\mathrm{CHCl}_{3}$ -DMF were almost the same with that of 1 (298 K). These results further confirmed that the MOF is dynamic in nature and 85 the transformation of the flexible framework is reversible,33,37,39,43 which also supported by the PXRD patterns. As shown in Fig. S6, the PXRD of solvent-exchanged 1 is different from that of 1, but return to 1 after immersion in DMF for $24\mathrm{h}$ (Fig. S7).
## $\mathbf{Pb}^{2+}$ sorption studies
The $\mathrm{\pH}$ value greatly influences the adsorption performance of $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ , so the effect of $\mathrm{\pH}$ ranging from 3.0 to 7.0 on the 15 adsorption of $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ was investigated. As shown in Fig. S8, the removal efficiency of 1a was very low at $\mathrm{pH}3.0$ and it increased dramatically with the increasing $\mathrm{\pH}$ and achieved the largest
signal at $\mathrm{pH}6.0$ . Further increasing $\mathrm{\pH}$ resulted in a decline of removal efficiency. This is due to the protonation effect on the 20 surface of the MOF adsorbent and excess $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{H}}^{+}$ competing for the sorption sites at low $\mathrm{\pH}$ . When $\mathrm{\pH}$ is higher than 6.0, $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ hydroxide precipitation may occur.45 Accordingly, the optimum $\mathrm{\pH}$ value of 6.0 was selected for the subsequent adsorption experiments.
25 To evaluate the effectiveness of $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ removing from water, 1a was placed in a $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ solution of $10\ \mathrm{ppm}$ at $\mathrm{pH}~6.0$ . The $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ loaded 1a (1a-Pb) was isolated and washed with water to remove the residual $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ on the exterior of 1a-Pb. Then it was examined by Energy-dispersive $\mathrm{\Delta}X$ -ray spectroscopy (EDS) and confirmed 30 the existence of $\mathrm{Pb}$ (Fig. S9). As shown in Fig. 2, 1a can rapidly capture $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ , and remove $98.12\%\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ within $7~\mathrm{min}$ . After $^\textrm{\scriptsize1h}$ , the concentration of the residual $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ reduced to $0.035\mathrm{ppm}$ , that is, $99.65\%$ $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ were removed. The fast kinetics and high efficiency for $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ removal could be attributed to the high 35 affinity of 1a.
To assess the sorbent’s affinity for $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ , the distribution coefficient $K_{d}(\mathrm{mL}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ was calculated as follows:
$$
K_{d}=\frac{\left(C_{0}-C_{e}\right)}{C_{e}}\times\frac{V}{m}
$$
where $C_{0}(\mathrm{mgL^{-1}})$ and $C_{e}(\mathrm{mgL}^{-1})$ are the initial and equilibrium 40 concentrations of $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ ; $V$ is the volume of the treated solution (mL); $m$ is the dry weight of adsorbent (g). The $K_{d}$ represents an important aspect of sorbent performance. In general, a material with a $K_{d}$ value above $10^{4}\mathrm{mLg^{-1}}$ is considered to be an excellent adsorbent.46,47 The $K_{d}$ value of 1a for $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ is $2.3\times10^{6}\mathrm{mL\g^{-1}}$ , 45 surpassing that of various reported benchmark materials, for examples, biochars $(10^{3}\mathrm{~-~}10^{4}\mathrm{~mL~g^{-1})}$ ,48 mesoporous carbon $(6.82\times10^{5}\mathrm{~mL~g^{-1})}$ ,49 commercial resins $(10^{4}-5.1\times10^{5}\mathrm{\mL}$ $\begin{array}{r}{\mathbf{g}^{-1},}\end{array}$ ),50 lignin functionalized carbon nanotubes $(3.6~\times~10^{5}~\mathrm{\mL}$ $\begin{array}{r}{\mathbf{g}^{-1},}\end{array}$ ),5 $^1\mathrm{MoS}_{4}–\mathrm{LDH}$ $(2.6\times10^{5}\mathrm{~mL~g^{-1})}$ ,47 pampeano aquifer $7.5\times$ 50 $10^{3}-1\times10^{4}\mathrm{mL\g^{-1})}$ .52
The adsorption isotherms were investigated to estimate the maximum adsorption capacity of 1a by varying the $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ concentrations from $5\ \mathrm{ppm}$ to $200~\mathrm{ppm}$ at $\mathrm{pH}6.0$ . As shown in Fig. 3a, the value of $q_{e}$ (equilibrium adsorption capacity) 5 increased with the increasing $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ concentrations and finally reached the maximum value of $616.64~\mathrm{mg~g^{-1}}$ . In addition, we also considered the removal efficiency at different ${\mathrm{Pb}}^{2+}$ initial concentrations (Fig. S10). More than $90\%\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ can be removed in the concentration range from 1 ppm to $75\mathrm{\ppm}$ . The 10 equilibrium adsorption isotherm data was fitted by Langmuir model, yielding a relative high correlation coefficient of 0.9997. The maximum adsorption capacity of 1a for $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ was calculated to be $613.50\quad\mathrm{mg}\quad\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , which closely matched with the experimental equilibrium value of $616.64~\mathrm{mg~g^{-1}}$ . We reasoned 15 that such an excellent lead adsorption capacity could be stemed from the highly accessible $\mathrm{o^{-}}$ groups densely populated throughout the pores of 1a.
To highlight the contribution of $\mathrm{o^{-}}$ groups to $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ uptake, we 25 synthesized a similar MOF as 1 but without $\mathrm{o^{-}}$ groups, $[\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{ADC})(\mathrm{BPE})_{0.5}]_{n}$ $(\mathrm{ADC}~=~4{,}4^{\prime}$ -azobenzenedicarboxylate)37
and investigated its adsorption isotherms for $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ . Result shows that the activated $[\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{ADC})(\mathrm{BPE})_{0.5}]_{n}$ displayed a lower uptake capacity of $473.92\ensuremath{\mathrm{~\mg~}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{~g}}^{-1}$ . Meanwhile, we calculated the 30 theoretical uptake capacity of 1a based on the molecular formula, pore volumes and one $0^{-}$ groups of per $\mathrm{L}^{2-}$ adsorbing one $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ by coordination interaction. The calculated theoretical uptake capacity is $470.11\mathrm{~mg~g^{-1}~}$ , which is obviously lower than the experimental equilibrium value $(616.64~\mathrm{~mg~}\mathrm{~g}^{-1})$ of 1a. The 35 higher experimental adsorption capacity for 1a could be attributed to the various adsorption modes formed by $\mathrm{o^{-}}$ groups, which offered not only coordination interactions but also electrostatic attractions between $0^{-}$ groups and $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ . To the best of our knowledge, the ${\mathrm{Pb}}^{2+}$ uptake capacity of 1a is the highest 40 among the reported MOF adsorbents (Table S3), and also exceeded that of other $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ adsorbents.51,53-57 These results suggest that 1a is a prospective adsorbent for $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ removal due to its large adsorption capacity and high removal efficiency.
Selective removal of heavy metal ions will facilitate 45 environmental protection and allow the reuse of heavy metals.53 However, a lot of adsorbents for $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ removal exhibit low selectivity. An absorbent with high selectivity is required to separate heavy metal ions from wastewater. Selectivity tests were performed in a mixed solution containing $\mathbf{Na}^{+}$ , $\ensuremath{\mathbf{M}}\ensuremath{\mathbf{g}}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{K}^{+}$ , $\mathrm{Ca}^{2+}$ , 50 $\mathrm{Mn}^{2+}$ , ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{Cd}^{2+}$ and $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ with the concentration of $10\:\mathrm{ppm}$ for each metal ion. The adsorption ability of 1a toward $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ is considerably higher than other ions (Fig. 4a). In the mixed solution, $99.27\%\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ were removed, while $17.46\%$ $\mathrm{Cd}^{2+}$ were absorbed. In contrast, other background metal ions $\mathrm{{Na}^{+}}$ , $\ensuremath{\mathbf{M}}\ensuremath{\mathbf{g}}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{K}^{+}$ 55 $\mathrm{Ca}^{2+}$ , $\ensuremath{\mathrm{Mn}}^{2+}$ , ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ do not quite bind to 1a with an equilibrium removal efficiency less than $8\%$ . This phenomenon can be explained by the HSAB principle.58 The negatively charged O atoms of the azoxy groups have the properties of a borderline base, which could readily bind with the metals ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ , 60 $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ (borderline acids) and $\mathrm{Cd}^{2+}$ (soft acid), and difficultly bind with $\mathrm{\DeltaNa}^{+}$ , $\ensuremath{\mathbf{M}}\ensuremath{\mathbf{g}}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{K}^{+}$ , $\mathrm{Ca}^{2+}$ , $\ensuremath{\mathrm{Mn}}^{2+}$ (hard acids). Compared to borderline acid $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ , the relative low removal efficiencies for borderline acids ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ and $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ are attributed to their fairly high hydration energy $(1915\mathrm{kJ\mol^{-1}}$ for ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ , $1980\mathrm{kJ\mol^{-1}}$ for $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ , 65 $1425~\mathrm{kJ~mol^{-1}}$ for $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ ).59 It is difficult for ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ and $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ to detach water molecules and further interact with the negatively charged O atoms. So the MOF material has a strong preference and fairly high selectivity for $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ against other metal ions, highlighting its potential in selective removing $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ from 70 practical wastewater.
$\mathrm{Ca}^{2+}$ and $\ensuremath{\mathbf{M}}\ensuremath{\mathbf{g}}^{2+}$ are common cations that always ubiquitously co-exist in natural water environment, and the same positivecharge and high level concentration might lead to competitive adsorption with $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+2,60,61}$ The soft acid $\mathrm{Cd}^{2+}$ , which get 75 comparable ionic size and charge density to $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ and often coexist with $\mathbf{Pb}^{2+62}$ can strongly compete for the selectivity of sorbents. Thus, it is essential to investigate the effect of competing ions $\mathrm{Ca}^{2+}$ , $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}^{2+}$ and $\mathrm{Cd}^{2+}$ on the adsorption of $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ . As shown in Fig. 4b, outstanding $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ removal efficiencies were 80 obtained in the presence of competing ions $\mathrm{Ca}^{2+}$ and $\ensuremath{\mathbf{M}}\ensuremath{\mathbf{g}}^{2+}$ . With the molar ratios of $\mathrm{Ca}^{2+}/\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ and $\mathrm{Mg^{2+}/P b^{2+}}$ ranging from 1 to 50, there were only little decrease for $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ removal efficiencies, changing from $99.82\%$ to $99.21\%$ with $\mathrm{Ca}^{2+}$ and $99.65\%$ to $99.31\%$ with $\ensuremath{\mathbf{M}}\ensuremath{\mathbf{g}}^{2+}$ . Large removal capacity $(96.43\%)$ was still obtained at a molar ratio of 10 for $\mathrm{Cd}^{2+}/\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ . When the ratio was 5 increased to 50, $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ removal efficiency reduced to $56.31\%$ . The concentration of $\mathrm{Cd}^{2+}$ in natural water environment can hardly reach such a high ratio, thus $\mathrm{Cd}^{2+}$ could not affect $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ removal performance in the practical application.
15 All of these results indicate that 1a has a strong preference and ultrahigh selectivity for $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ sorption. The particular selectivity can be ascribed to the following reasons: (1) the densely populated $\mathrm{o^{-}}$ groups of the functionalized MOF provided strong interactions between $\mathrm{o^{-}}$ and $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ ; (2) adsorbents preferentially 20 adsorb divalent cations with a lower hydration energy in general, as metal ions have to detach large share of hydration water before they enter the smaller channels of adsorbents.59,60,63,64 Compared with $\mathrm{Ca}^{2+}$ $1505\mathrm{kJ\mol^{-1}};$ ), $\mathbf{Mg}^{2+}(1830\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{-1})$ and $\mathrm{Cd}^{2+}$ (1755 $\mathrm{\kJ\mol^{-1}}.$ ), the lowest Gibbs free energy of hydration was 25 observed for $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ $(1425~\mathrm{kJ~mol^{-1}})$ ), indicating $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ preferential adsorption than other divalent cations;59 (3) It has been reported that larger ionic radius would be favorable for the interactions between metal ions and functional groups of the adsorbent.65-67 The radiuses of $\mathrm{Ca}^{2+}$ , $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}^{2+}$ and $\mathrm{Cd}^{2+}$ are $0.100\mathrm{nm}$ , $0.072{\mathrm{~nm}}$ and 30 $0.095\mathrm{nm}$ , respectively, while the radius of $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ is $0.119\mathrm{nm}$ . The larger radius would endow 1a with quite good selectivity for $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ adsorption.
## Stability and reusability study
Stability and reusability are two important factors in the 35 application of adsorbent. To verify the chemical stability of 1a, we performed in situ PXRD measurements to evaluate the crystalline structure. In Figs. 5 and S11, after soaking 1a in water for $300\ \mathrm{min}$ , even soaking in $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ solution more than $300\ \mathrm{min}$ , it still holds high crystalline form and retained the structure of 1a, 40 indicating the good chemical stability of 1a in water and $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ solution. Further immersion of 1a in aqueous solution of $\mathrm{pH}3.0\$ - 10.0 $300\mathrm{min},$ ), its high crystalline form were also preserved (Fig. S12). Meanwhile, SEM investigations were carried out to examine the surface morphology of 1a before and after loading 45 $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ . As shown in Fig. S13b, the crystals of 1a did not collapse during the sorption process.36 The adsorption isotherm of 1a-Pb exhibits negligible uptake for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ (Fig. S14). The different adsorption capacities and behaviors of 1a and $\mathbf{1a-Pb}$ are clearly caused by loading $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ . To get single crystals with better quality, 50 we soaked 1-Pb in DMF for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ , and determined the unit cell parameters of 1-Pb-DMF (Table S1). By comparison of the unit cell parameters with 1 (298 K), we can affirm that the adsorbent still maintain the single crystallinity even after loading $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ .
From the consideration of economical and practical, it is of great significance to study the desorption and regeneration of an adsorbent. As shown in Fig. 6, 1a exhibited a favorable cycle performance for $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ adsorption, and the removal efficiency 60 remained almost unchanged in the first two cycles and only slight fading was observed in the following cycles. The slight decrease may result from the loss of the adsorbent during cycling. Similar results were also reported in previous studies.55,68.
## Mechanism of $\mathbf{Pb}^{2+}$ removal
Generally, the adsorptive modes for metal ions removal mainly 5 include electrostatic adsorption, coordination adsorption and ion exchange. To investigate whether there is electrostatic attraction in the sorption process of $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ , the zeta potential measurements were performed. When the $\mathrm{\pH}$ was above 4.0, the surface of 1a was negatively charged, and the density of negative charge 10 increased remarkably as $\mathsf{p H}$ value increasing (Fig. S15). Meanwhile, the removal efficiency exhibited a similar variation tendency along with the change of $\mathrm{\pH}$ value. The zeta potential of 1a-Pb appeared to be less negative in the $\mathrm{\pH}$ range of 5.0-7.0 due to charge neutralization. Hence, it is believed that the 15 electrostatic attraction is the main force making the adsorption of $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ onto 1a. When the solution $\mathrm{\pH}$ was below 4.0, the surface charge on 1a turned to positively charged, resulting in electrostatic repulsion between 1a and $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ . However, 1a still exhibited a removal efficiency of $19.68\%$ at $\mathrm{\pH}$ of 3.0, which 20 demonstrate that there are other interactions besides the electrostatic effect.
Evaluating the effect of background electrolyte on $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ sorption is an effective macroscopic method to understand the adsorption mechanism.69 One can see that the background 25 electrolyte had little effect on $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ adsorption (Fig. S16). Even in high concentration of $500\mathrm{ppm}$ , a relative high removal efficiency $(98.39\%)$ for $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ was still obtained. Therefore, the mechanism of $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ removal does not involve ion exchange behavior.
To further investigate the mechanism of $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ sorption, FT-IR 30 spectra of 1a and 1a-Pb were studied. In Fig. 7, a new peak at $581~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ for 1a-Pb is a typical characteristic stretching vibration of $\mathrm{Pb-O},^{\mathrm{7(}}$ confirming the fact of $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ loaded. The bands at 1378 $\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and $798~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ occur after $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ adsorption, implying the presence of ${\mathrm{NO}}_{3}{}^{-}$ , for charge balance.47,71,72 There are strong 35 interactions between $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ and $\mathrm{o^{-}}$ groups that could limit N–O stretching vibrations and consequently decrease their vibration frequency, giving rise to a red shift from $1223~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ to 1218 $\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ .25 For 1a, the peaks at $1608~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ , $1579\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and $1375~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ are assigned to the characteristic groups of the aromatic $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C=C}}$ and 40 $\mathrm{C-N}$ vibrations,73 whereas these peaks shifted to $1601~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ , 1573 $\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , $1363~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ after loading $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ . This might give further evidence of coordination interactions between $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ and $_{0}$ atoms.
## Conclusions
In summary, a novel $Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{II})$ -based MOF functionalized with $\mathrm{o^{-}}$ groups was elaborately constructed for the removal of $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ from aqueous solution. The activated MOF showed excellent 5 performance for $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ removal with a record-high uptake capacity of $616.64~\mathrm{~mg~}\mathrm{~g}^{-1}$ among MOF adsorbents, high removal efficiency in a wide range $(>90\%$ at $1{-}75~\mathrm{ppm})$ and exceptional distribution coefficient value of $2.3\times10^{6}\mathrm{\mL\}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , making the activated MOF one of the most promising materials for 10 eliminating $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ pollution from water. Notably, the activated MOF can selectively capture $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ with high efficiency even in the presence of high concentration of other metal ions. Also, the adsorbent can be readily regenerated and recycled without significant decrease of removal efficiency. The densely populated 15 and highly accessible $0^{-}$ groups with their remarkable affinity for $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ are responsible for the impressive results. The work therefore lays a foundation for introducing charged groups into MOFs as a new platform for efficiently removing contaminants from aqueous solution.
